    ("Duchamp: Bicycle Wheel", "Marcel Duchamp", 1913, "Bicycle wheel on stool (replica)", "Dada / Readymade", ["readymade","first readymade","conceptual","wheel","rotation"], ["bicycle wheel","stool","rotation","first readymade","concept"], ["chrome wheel","wooden stool"], "Museum of Modern Art, MoMA", "A bicycle wheel mounted upside-down on a white kitchen stool.", "Duchamp's first readymade — chosen for visual pleasure (the spinning wheel) rather than meaning. The concept of the readymade would change art forever.", "male", "French"),
    ("Duchamp: In Advance of the Broken Arm", "Marcel Duchamp", 1915, "Snow shovel (replica)", "Dada / Readymade", ["readymade","snow shovel","conceptual","anti-art","concept"], ["snow shovel","hardware store","signed","readymade","titled"], ["galvanized steel grey","wooden handle"], "Yale University Art Gallery", "A standard hardware-store snow shovel, purchased unmodified, titled and signed as artwork.", "The first American readymade. Duchamp's question: if an artist designates an object as art, does the object become art? Still unanswered.", "male", "French"),
    ("Warhol: Brillo Box", "Andy Warhol", 1964, "Silkscreen on plywood", "Pop Art / American", ["pop art","brand","consumer culture","product","serial"], ["Brillo soap pad box","silkscreen","stacked","consumer brand"], ["red","blue","white","consumer brand colors"], "Various museums", "Wooden boxes painted and silkscreened to replicate Brillo soap pad packaging exactly.", "Arthur Danto asked: if the Brillo Box looks identical to what's in the supermarket, what makes the art world's version Art? His answer launched 'institutional theory'.", "male", "American"),
    ("Warhol: Disaster Series (Electric Chair)", "Andy Warhol", 1963, "Acrylic and silkscreen on canvas", "Pop Art / American", ["pop art","death","repetition","electric chair","dark"], ["electric chair","empty room","silkscreen","repetition","death"], ["grey","dark purple","muted","death"], "Museum of Modern Art, MoMA", "A photograph of an empty electric chair, silkscreened in grey on a near-monochrome canvas.", "Warhol's 'Death and Disaster' series addresses the mass media's numbing effect on images of death. The empty chair is more disturbing than an occupied one.", "male", "American"),
    ("Warhol: Mao", "Andy Warhol", 1972, "Acrylic and silkscreen on canvas", "Pop Art / American", ["pop art","political","silkscreen","celebrity","colorful"], ["Mao Zedong","silkscreen portrait","colorful warhol treatment","political icon"], ["vivid pop colors","silkscreened face"], "Art Institute of Chicago", "The official portrait of Mao Zedong treated with Warhol's silkscreen and vivid pop colors.", "Warhol made Mao portraits immediately after Nixon's visit to China in 1972. The treatment elevates/deflates political authority into celebrity.", "male", "American"),
    ("Beuys: I Like America and America Likes Me", "Joseph Beuys", 1974, "Performance (photograph documentation)", "Conceptual Art / German", ["performance","political","coyote","duration","healing"], ["Beuys","wild coyote","felt blanket","staff","isolation"], ["grey felt","brown coyote","documentation photo"], "René Block Gallery, New York", "Beuys spent three days in a gallery room with a wild coyote — his felt-wrapped staff the only mediating object.", "A healing ritual addressing America's treatment of indigenous peoples. The coyote is a sacred figure to Native Americans. Beuys arrived and departed without touching American ground.", "male", "German"),
    ("Beuys: The Pack (Das Rudel)", "Joseph Beuys", 1969, "Volkswagen van, 24 sledges with felt, fat, flashlights", "Conceptual Art / German", ["installation","felt","fat","survival kit","political"], ["VW van","sledges","felt","fat","survival","emergency"], ["grey felt brown","industrial grey"], "Neue Galerie, Kassel", "A Volkswagen van with 24 sledges pouring from its back doors, each equipped with felt, fat, and a flashlight — a survival kit.", "Beuys linked the materials to his own WWII survival story (he says he was wrapped in felt and fat by Tartar nomads after his plane crashed). The survival kit as political statement.", "male", "German"),
    ("Kiefer: The Rhine", "Anselm Kiefer", 1982, "Oil, acrylic, shellac, straw on canvas", "Neo-Expressionism / German", ["expressionist","landscape","historical","german","dark"], ["Rhine landscape","leaden sky","straw","desolation","Germany"], ["grey sky","dark earth","straw texture","dark"], "Centre Pompidou, Paris", "A leaden landscape with a dark river and a horizon weighted with the materiality of straw and paint.", "Kiefer's Rhine invokes the Romantic mythology of Germany — Caspar David Friedrich — but blackened with historical guilt.", "male", "German"),
    ("Baselitz: Dresdner Frauen (Dresden Women)", "Georg Baselitz", 1989, "Oil on canvas", "Neo-Expressionism / German", ["expressionist","inverted","female figures","historical","bold"], ["inverted women figures","Dresden","bold paint","upside down"], ["vivid color","inverted figures","raw paint"], "Various collections", "Monumental female figures painted upside-down in raw, expressionist color.", "Baselitz began inverting his figures in 1969 to separate form from content — the painting becomes about paint, not subject matter.", "male", "German"),
    ("Lüpertz: Germania", "Markus Lüpertz", 1970, "Distemper on canvas", "Neo-Expressionism / German", ["expressionist","german mythology","bold","symbolic","painting"], ["Germania allegory","German helmet","bold form","symbolic"], ["dark earth","grey","dramatic"], "Various collections", "A German military helmet motif as abstracted symbolic form.", "Lüpertz's Germania paintings confront German history through the recycled iconography of militarism — helmets as pure paint form.", "male", "German"),
    ("Immendorff: Café Deutschland", "Jörg Immendorff", 1977, "Oil on canvas", "Neo-Expressionism / German", ["expressionist","political","divided Germany","bar scene","allegorical"], ["café","east west germany","figures","political allegory","eagle"], ["dark","vivid color","figures dense","political"], "Städel Museum, Frankfurt", "An allegorical bar scene where East and West German figures meet/clash across a divided table.", "Immendorff's Café Deutschland series addressed the division of Germany through baroque political allegory. A unique fusion of art history and political commentary.", "male", "German"),
    ("Richter: Ema (Nude on a Staircase)", "Gerhard Richter", 1966, "Oil on canvas", "Photorealism / German", ["photorealist","blurred","nude","staircase","duchamp reference"], ["nude woman descending staircase","blurred photograph","photorealism"], ["grey","flesh","blurred photo quality"], "Museum Ludwig, Cologne", "A nude woman descends a staircase in a blurred photographic style — a homage to Duchamp.", "Richter's most direct reference to art history. The blurring transforms a domestic photograph into an investigation of seeing and representation.", "male", "German"),
    # EAST ASIAN EXPANDED
    ("Ni Zan: The Rongxi Studio", "Ni Zan", 1372, "Ink on paper scroll", "Yuan Dynasty / Chinese", ["literati","ink","spare","three zones","minimalist"], ["bamboo","empty pavilion","distant mountains","three zones","minimal"], ["pale ink wash","white paper","spare"], "National Palace Museum, Taipei", "Sparse rocks and bamboo, an empty pavilion, and distant mountains in three horizontal zones of pale ink.", "Ni Zan's radical emptiness — no figures, no activity, a conspicuously absent human world — became the summit of literati painting.", "male", "Chinese"),
